<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">ʻAi Noa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1819">1819</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The ʻAi Noa, literally free eating, was a period of taboo-breaking in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaiian Islands</a> in October 1819 that overturned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapu-hawaiian-culture">kapu</a>, the strict rules of Hawaiian religion and society. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a>, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-ii">Liholiho</a>, later Kamehameha II, tried to reimpose the kapu suspended during mourning, but was opposed by his mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keopuolani">Keōpūolani</a>, Kamehameha's other wives including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ka-ahumanu">Kaʻahumanu</a>, and the kahuna-nui <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hewahewa">Hewahewa</a>. He then ate a feast the chiefesses had prepared, and messengers announced that the kapu had fallen: women could eat forbidden food and eat with men, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a> was to end. A revolt led by Keaoua Kekuaokalani was defeated at the Battle of Kuamo'o in December 1819.</p></div>
